  "evidence": "TEXT_G: Leave the EU to restore sovereign decision-making and end rule by unelected EU bodies; immediate five‑year freeze on permanent immigration, later cap ~50,000, no amnesty, loyalty undertakings, end official multiculturalism, replace the Human Rights Act and leave the ECHR; victims‑first justice with “life means life,” three‑strikes, doubled prison places, youth boot camps, withdraw from the European Arrest Warrant; counter extremist Islam (ban burqa/niqab in public buildings, deport radical preachers), reaffirm UK law over sharia; civic nationalism, support the monarchy and Church of England; repeal the Climate Change Act.\nTEXT_E: Managed immigration with an annual cap on non‑EU migrants and promotion of integration; tougher knife sentences, elected police commissioners, Border Police Force; EU stance is positive but guarded (no new powers without a referendum, Sovereignty Bill, repatriate some powers); family‑friendly policies including recognising marriage in tax; alongside strong civil‑liberties measures (scrap ID cards, curb surveillance) and a proactive green agenda (Green Investment Bank, carbon price floor) and internationalist aid commitments (honour 0.7% ODA).",
  "reasoning": "TEXT_G argues from strong TAN premises—national sovereignty over supranational authority, restrictive immigration framed around identity and order, rejection of multiculturalism, tough law‑and‑order, and protection of traditional institutions. TEXT_E mixes some TAN themes with significant civil‑liberties, green, and internationalist commitments, making it less aligned with a strongly TAN (0.85) profile.",
